# Which terminal xdg-terminal-exec opens.
#
# env-hypr's launch-tui does not name a terminal: it calls xdg-terminal-exec so
# that --app-id is translated into whatever flag the chosen terminal uses. With
# no list here that choice falls back to scanning the desktop entries for
# Categories=TerminalEmulator, and on a machine that also has kitty installed
# kitty wins - so SUPER+SHIFT+T opened btop in kitty, not ghostty, with none of
# the ghostty config in env-common applying to it.
#
# First installed entry wins; kitty stays as the fallback for a machine without
# ghostty. Both declare X-TerminalArgAppId, so the zepzeper.* window rules in
# env-hypr keep matching either way.
com.mitchellh.ghostty.desktop
kitty.desktop
